<article class="env-lemma" id="07U4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07U4">Lemma <span data-tag="07U4">67.49.5</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. For every algebraic space $X$ over $S$ satisfying the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BB1" data-tag="0BB1">67.49.1</a> there exists a morphism of algebraic spaces </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \nu _ X : X^\nu \longrightarrow X  \]
</div>
  <p> with the following properties </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
if $X$ satisfies the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BB1" data-tag="0BB1">67.49.1</a> then $X^\nu $ is normal and $\nu _ X$ is integral, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $X$ is a scheme such that every quasi-compact open has finitely many irreducible components, then $\nu _ X : X^\nu \to X$ is the normalization of $X$ constructed in Morphisms, Section <a href="/tag/035E" data-tag="035E">29.55</a>, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $f : X \to Y$ is a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$ which both satisfy the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BB1" data-tag="0BB1">67.49.1</a> and every codimension $0$ point of $X$ is mapped by $f$ to a codimension $0$ point of $Y$, then there is a unique morphism $f^\nu : X^\nu \to Y^\nu $ of algebraic spaces over $S$ such that $\nu _ Y \circ f^\nu = f \circ \nu _ X$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $f : X \to Y$ is an étale or smooth morphism of algebraic spaces and $Y$ satisfies the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BB1" data-tag="0BB1">67.49.1</a>, then the hypotheses of (3) hold and the morphism $f^\nu $ induces an isomorphism $X^\nu \to X \times _ Y Y^\nu $. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider the category $\mathcal{C}$ whose objects are the schemes $U$ over $S$ such that every quasi-compact open of $U$ has finitely many irreducible components and whose morphisms are those morphisms $g : U \to V$ of schemes over $S$ such that every generic point of an irreducible component of $U$ is mapped to the generic point of an irreducible component of $V$. We have already shown that 
    <p>
      <ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(a)"><p>
for $U \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ we have a normalization morphism $\nu _ U : U^\nu \to U$ as in Morphisms, Definition <a href="/tag/035N" data-tag="035N">29.55.1</a>, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(b)"><p>
for $U \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ the morphism $\nu _ U$ is integral and $U^\nu $ is a normal scheme, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/035Q" data-tag="035Q">29.55.5</a>, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(c)"><p>
for every $g : U \to V \in \text{Arrows}(\mathcal{C})$ there is a unique morphism $g^\nu : U^\nu \to V^\nu $ such that $\nu _ V \circ g^\nu = g \circ \nu _ U$, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/035Q" data-tag="035Q">29.55.5</a> part (4) applied to the composition $X^\nu \to X \to Y$, 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(d)"><p>
if $V \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ and $g : U \to V$ is étale or smooth, then $U \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ and $g \in \text{Arrows}(\mathcal{C})$ and the morphism $g^\nu $ induces an isomorphism $U^\nu \to U \times _ V V^\nu $, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0GMD" data-tag="0GMD">67.49.4</a> and More on Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/07TD" data-tag="07TD">37.19.3</a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol>
    <p>
       Our task is to extend this construction to the corresponding category of algebraic spaces $X$ over $S$. 
    <p>
      Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$ satisfying the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BB1" data-tag="0BB1">67.49.1</a>. Let $U \to X$ be a surjective étale morphism where $U$ is a scheme. Set $R = U \times _ X U$ with projections $s, t : R \to U$ and $j = (t, s) : R \to U \times _ S U$ so that $X = U/R$, see Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0262" data-tag="0262">65.9.1</a>. Observe that $U$ and $R$ are objects of $\mathcal{C}$ by our assumptions on $X$ and that the morphisms $s$ and $t$ are étale morphisms of schemes over $S$. By (a) we have the normalization morphisms $\nu _ U : U^\nu \to U$ and $\nu _ R : R^\nu \to R$, by (d) we have morphisms $s^\nu : R^\nu \to U^\nu $, $t^\nu : R^\nu \to U^\nu $ which define isomorphisms $R^\nu \to R \times _{s, U} U^\nu $ and $R^\nu \to U^\nu \times _{U, t} R$. It follows that $s^\nu $ and $t^\nu $ are étale (as they are isomorphic to base changes of étale morphisms). The induced morphism $j^\nu = (t^\nu , s^\nu ) : R^\nu \to U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu $ is a monomorphism as it is equal to the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  R^\nu &  \to (U^\nu \times _{U, t} R) \times _ R (R \times _{s, U} U^\nu ) \\ &  = U^\nu \times _{U, t} R \times _{s, U} U^\nu \\ &  \xrightarrow {j} U^\nu \times _ U (U \times _ S U) \times _ U U^\nu \\ &  = U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu \end{align*}
</div>
    <p>
       The first arrow is the diagonal morphism of $\nu _ R$. (This tells us that $R^\nu $ is a subscheme of the restriction of $R$ to $U^\nu $.) A formal computation with fibre products using property (d) shows that $R^\nu \times _{s^\nu , U^\nu , t^\nu } R^\nu $ is the normalization of $R \times _{s, U, t} R$. Hence the étale morphism $c : R \times _{s, U, t} R \to R$ extends uniquely to $c^\nu $ by (d). The morphism $c^\nu $ is compatible with the projection $\text{pr}_{13} : U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu \to U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu $. Similarly, there are morphisms $i^\nu : R^\nu \to R^\nu $ compatible with the morphism $U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu \to U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu $ which switches factors and there is a morphism $e^\nu : U^\nu \to R^\nu $ compatible with the diagonal morphism $U^\nu \to U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu $. All in all it follows that $j^\nu : R^\nu \to U^\nu \times _ S U^\nu $ is an étale equivalence relation. At this point we may and do set $X^\nu = U^\nu /R^\nu $ (Spaces, Theorem <a href="/tag/02WW" data-tag="02WW">65.10.5</a>). Then we see that we have $U^\nu = X^\nu \times _ X U$ by Groupoids, Lemma <a href="/tag/07S3" data-tag="07S3">39.20.7</a>. 
    <p>
      What have we shown in the previous paragraph is this: for every algebraic space $X$ over $S$ satisfying the equivalent conditions of Lemma <a href="/tag/0BB1" data-tag="0BB1">67.49.1</a> if we choose a surjective étale morphism $g : U \to X$ where $U$ is a scheme, then we obtain a cartesian diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X^\nu \ar[d]_{\nu _ X} &  U^\nu \ar[l]^{g^\nu } \ar[d]^{\nu _ U} \\  X &  U \ar[l]_ g }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of algebraic spaces. This immediately implies that $X^\nu $ is a normal algebraic space and that $\nu _ X$ is an integral morphism. This gives part (1) of the lemma. 
    <p>
      We will show below that the morphism $\nu _ X : X^\nu \to X$ up to unique isomorphism is independent of the choice of $g$, but for now, if $X$ is a scheme, we choose $\text{id} : X \to X$ so that it is clear that we have part (2) of the lemma. 
    <p>
      We still have to prove parts (3) and (4). Let $g : U \to X$ and $\nu _ X : X^\nu \to X$ and $g^\nu : U^\nu \to X^\nu $ be as above. Let $Z$ be a normal scheme and let $h : Z \to U$ and $a : Z \to X^\nu $ be morphisms over $S$ such that $g \circ h = \nu _ X \circ a$ and such that every irreducible component of $Z$ dominates an irreducible component of $U$ (via $h$). By Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/035Q" data-tag="035Q">29.55.5</a> part (4) we obtain a unique morphism $h^\nu : Z \to U^\nu $ such that $h = \nu _ U \circ h^\nu $. Picture: 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X^\nu \ar[d]_{\nu _ X} &  U^\nu \ar[l]^{g^\nu } \ar[d]^{\nu _ U} &  Z \ar[l]^{h^\nu } \ar@/_1em/[ll]_ a \ar[dl]^ h \\  X &  U \ar[l]_ g }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Observe that $a = g^\nu \circ h^\nu $. Namely, since the square with corners $X^\nu $, $X$, $U^\nu $, $U$ is cartesian, this follows immediately from the fact that $h^\nu $ is unique (given $h$). In other words, given $h : Z \to U$ as above (and not $a$) there is a unique morphism $a : Z \to X^\nu $ with $\nu _ X \circ a = g \circ h$. 
    <p>
      Let $f : X \to Y$ be as in part (3) of the statement of the lemma. Suppose we have chosen surjective étale morphisms $U \to X$ and $V \to Y$ where $U$ and $V$ are schemes such that $f$ lifts to a morphism $g : U \to V$. Then $g \in \text{Arrows}(\mathcal{C})$ and we obtain a unique morphism $g^\nu : U^\nu \to V^\nu $ compatible with $\nu _ U$ and $\nu _ V$. However, then the two morphisms 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R^\nu = U^\nu \times _{X^\nu } U^\nu \to U^\nu \to V^\nu \to Y^\nu  \]
</div>
    <p>
       must be the same by our comments in the previous paragraph (applied with $Y$ in stead of $X$). Since $X^\nu $ is constructed by taking the quotient of $U^\nu $ by $R^\nu $ it follows that we obtain a (unique) morphism $f^\nu : X^\nu \to Y^\nu $ as stated in (3). 
    <p>
      To see that the construction of $X^\nu $ is independent of the choice of $g : U \to X$ surjective étale, apply the construction in the previous paragraph to $\text{id} : X \to X$ and a morphism $U' \to U$ between étale coverings of $X$. This is enough because given any two étale coverings of $X$ there is a third one which dominates both. The reader shows that the morphism between the two normalizations constructed using either $U' \to X$ or $U \to X$ becomes an isomorphism after base change to $U'$ and hence was an isomorphism. We omit the details. 
    <p>
      We omit the proof of (4) which is similar; hint use part (d) above. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>